WEBVTT

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:10.330
Usually, when you look at a corporate balance sheet, you expect the math to tell a coherent

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:11.330
story.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:12.330
Right.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:13.570
Like a single version of the truth.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:14.570
Exactly.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:16.730
It functions almost like a blueprint for the business.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:19.970
The money comes in, the money goes out, and if there's a fundamental flaw in the

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.850
operation, it's just sitting right there in black and white.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:26.010
I mean, it's supposed to be an objective accounting of reality.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.450
You know, a ledger shouldn't have hidden agendas.

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:34.050
But then you step into the world of healthcare financing, specifically in Puerto Rico,

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.730
and suddenly that balance sheet turns into a total Rorschach test.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:38.730
Oh, absolutely.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:39.730
It's wild.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.930
We're looking at a financial landscape today that is full of massive glaring contradictions.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:48.170
Like two opposing sides are looking at the exact same numbers and drawing wildly different

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:49.290
conclusions.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:53.170
It represents the absolute definition of a fiscal paradox.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:54.770
Welcome to today's deep dive.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:59.250
Our mission today is to unpack this really fascinating 2026 study published in Health

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.250
Affair Scholar.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:01.250
Yeah.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:06.090
It looks at the financial realities of Medicare Advantage, which we'll call MA, down in Puerto

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:07.090
Rico.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:11.730
The hook here, the thing that makes us so compelling for you listening, is this massive

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.450
contradiction playing out right now.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:14.450
Right.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:18.490
Because on one hand you have insurance companies in Puerto Rico sounding the alarm.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:23.130
They claim they are severely underfunded by the federal government operating on razor

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.170
thin margins and that they desperately need more money just to survive.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:31.810
But then on the other hand, you have this study and critics looking at the system trying

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:36.090
to argue that these companies are operating as a highly profitable oligopoly.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.090
Yeah.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:40.850
They claim it's sustained by public funds while constantly demanding more.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:44.730
But I think we really need to question the premise of this study today.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.530
Oh, we definitely do.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:50.850
Because when you actually look at the facts, and we will, the narrative this study

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:53.250
pushes gets a little shaky.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.730
To understand the stakes here, we have to set the table with just how unique

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:59.610
the healthcare landscape in Puerto Rico actually is.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:03.410
The structure of the market is entirely different than the mainland.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:04.410
Right.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:10.130
Just for context, if you look at the U.S. mainland in 2024, about 54% of Medicare beneficiaries

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.850
are enrolled in a Medicare Advantage plan.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.210
Which is private managed care.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:17.730
You know, where the government pays a private company to manage a patient's health.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.170
As opposed to traditional Medicare, which is run directly by the government.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:25.570
So on the mainland, 54% is a slight majority.

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:32.010
But in Puerto Rico, a staggering 95% of Medicare beneficiaries are enrolled in MA plans.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:33.010
95%.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.290
It's not just an alternative option.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:39.650
It is the de facto healthcare system for older adults on the archipelago.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.490
It is total market dominance.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:46.350
And if you pay a monthly premium, you probably assume that money sits in a vault waiting

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:47.810
for the day you get sick.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:48.810
Right.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:53.370
But when private companies control 95% of an entire demographic's healthcare, that

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:57.210
premium becomes part of a completely different game.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:01.730
You're going to want to hear how the math of your health actually works behind closed doors.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:05.810
Because what happens in a market with that level of penetration acts as a crystal ball

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.130
for the rest of the country.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:08.130
Okay.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:09.130
Let's unpack this.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:12.890
To figure out who is right in this debate, we first have to understand the specific

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:16.170
funding gap that these insurers are complaining about.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:19.290
The insurers aren't just making up numbers out of thin air, are they?

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:20.290
No.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.290
Not at all.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:23.690
The gap is a documented reality.

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:28.050
Because of Puerto Rico's status as a U.S. territory rather than a state, the federal

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.130
funding formulas work differently.

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:31.130
Right.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:35.210
Specifically, the federal risk standardized benchmark payments, which is basically the

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:40.490
baseline amount the government pays a plan to cover a person, those payments to MA plans

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:45.530
in Puerto Rico are approximately 38% lower than they are in the U.S. states.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:48.570
38% lower is a massive haircut.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:49.570
It's huge.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:53.130
It's exactly the argument from the Medicaid and Medicare Advantage Product Association

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:54.130
of Puerto Rico.

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:57.210
They represent the big health insurance companies down there.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:02.770
And they argue that this 38% underfunding creates intense financial instability.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:06.730
They've stated this disparity forces them into a corner where they have to consider

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:11.130
reducing health care benefits for patients and they're demanding more federal funds.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:15.130
I mean, on its face, that argument follows a logical business progression.

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:17.610
Less revenue should equal less stability.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:18.930
I have to push back here though.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:23.650
If any business is receiving 38% less revenue than its peers on the mainland to do the exact

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:28.730
same job, it seems like it would naturally be struggling just to keep the lights on.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:33.970
So how are they staying afloat, let alone allegedly operating as this profitable oligopoly

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:34.970
the study claims?

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.450
Well, that brings us to the nuance of the data.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:41.210
The 38% number is real, but it is not the final math.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:42.450
Okay, explain that.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:46.530
The study highlights two crucial mitigating factors that act as a counterbalance.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:51.530
First, these insurance plans receive higher risk scores and quality bonus payments from

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:52.530
the federal government.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.970
Wait, how does an insurer just get a higher risk score for a patient?

00:04:55.970 --> 00:05:00.210
Are people in Puerto Rico just fundamentally sicker or is this an accounting trick?

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:04.490
It's a mix of demographics, but a significant portion comes down to what the industry calls

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:07.130
coding intensity.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:10.970
Medicare pays these private insurers more money if a patient is deemed sicker and

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:12.610
more complex to treat.

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:18.530
So insurers are highly incentivized to comb through medical records, sometimes hiring massive

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:19.930
teams to do so.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:22.330
Just to add diagnostic codes to a patient's file.

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:23.330
Exactly.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:27.290
If they find an unrecorded minor condition and code it, the patient's risk score goes

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:30.210
up and the federal government sends a bigger check.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:34.930
So they are maximizing the paper trail to maximize the revenue.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:37.370
They are very efficient at it.

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:42.090
And when you factor in those elevated risk scores and quality bonuses, they partially

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:43.930
offset that initial gap.

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:47.810
It reduces the actual difference in federal payments to roughly 26%.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:51.030
26% is still a significant gap though.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:54.810
We are chipping away at the original 38%, but it's still a big hole.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:55.810
It is.

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:59.970
But here's the second factor, and this is the vital piece of the puzzle.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:03.570
The cost of doing business in Puerto Rico is drastically different.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:04.570
Right.

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:08.010
Specifically, Medicare reimbursement rates for the physicians actually providing

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:13.090
the physical care in Puerto Rico are 40% lower than in the US states.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:15.090
Wait, let me make sure I have this straight.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:19.810
The federal government pays the insurers 26% less than they would state side.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:24.290
But the insurers turn around and pay the local doctors 40% less?

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:26.570
You've hit on the exact buffer zone.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:30.610
The doctors are taking a much bigger relative pay cut than the insurance companies are

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:33.130
taking a funding cut.

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:36.930
That 14% differential creates a massive financial cushion.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:43.010
It leaves room for insurers to maintain profitability even under relative federal underfunding simply

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:46.090
by passing the squeeze down to the actual healthcare providers.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:49.730
Okay, so the funding gap alone clearly doesn't tell the whole story.

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:54.210
If we really want to know if these corporations are struggling or thriving, we have to actually

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:57.250
open up their books and follow the money.

00:06:57.250 --> 00:07:01.770
That is exactly what the researchers of this Health Affairs Scholar study did.

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:06.890
They built a consolidated data set, analyzing financial statements from 2011 all the way to

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:07.890
2022.

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:09.850
A full 12 years of receipts.

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:14.170
Yeah, and they focused on the three massive insurers that essentially run the board down

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:15.170
there.

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:17.850
MMM, Triple-S, and MCS.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:23.570
By 2022, these three companies controlled about 92% of all Medicare beneficiaries

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:25.090
in Puerto Rico.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:29.770
With mean annual revenues of $4.92 billion.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:33.810
$4.92 billion a year is a staggering pool of money.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:37.970
Now the study breaks down those financials into three core metrics to track the flow of

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:38.970
that cash.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:40.770
Let's walk through those.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:44.810
First, there is the medical loss ratio or MLR.

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:49.290
This is the percentage of revenue actually spent on medical care, doctors, hospitals,

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:50.290
prescriptions.

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:51.290
Right.

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:54.770
Second, there is the administrative loss ratio or ALR.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:58.890
This is the money spent on bureaucracy, marketing, executive salaries.

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:00.450
And third, you have the profit margins.

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:02.490
What is left over for the house?

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:06.410
To understand the tension between these three, we have to talk about a crucial piece of federal

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:07.570
legislation.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:08.570
The Affordable Care Act.

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:10.070
Yes, the ACA.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:15.970
In 2014, the ACA implemented an 85% MLR floor for these Medicare Advantage plans.

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:20.770
To put it simply by federal law, 85 cents of every single federal dollar these companies

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:24.810
take in must go to actual patient care and provider payments.

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:25.810
Exactly.

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:29.530
They're really capped at using the remaining 15 cents for their administrative costs and

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:30.530
profits.

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:35.530
And that floor was designed to ensure that taxpayer money actually funds health care rather

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:38.210
than just financing corporate bloat.

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:42.490
But here is where we really have to pause and look at this study with a bit of a cynical

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:43.490
eye.

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:44.490
I agree completely.

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:48.370
Because the study points out that the funding disparity wasn't significantly associated

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:50.790
with the medical loss ratio.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:55.290
The researchers frame this as some sort of chaotic legality or market failure.

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:58.130
They act like it's a huge scandal.

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:00.570
But wait, state the true facts here.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:05.650
Out of every dollar these plans get, more than 85 cents is going directly to patient

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:07.690
benefits and provider payments, right?

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:08.690
Yes.

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:10.410
They are complying with the statute.

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.530
They are hitting the MLR floor.

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:16.490
And on top of that, if you look at how CMS, the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:19.770
Services, if you look at how they rate these plans...

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:22.290
Oh, this is the craziest part.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:27.570
99% of all Medicare Advantage recipients in Puerto Rico are on plans that are rated

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:30.370
4.5 stars or more by CMS.

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:34.410
99% on 4.5 star plans.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:39.330
So what exactly is the illegality or the chaos this study is trying to point out?

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:41.410
That is the million dollar question.

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:45.210
If you compare these health plans in Puerto Rico to the ones in the mainland U.S.,

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:48.650
according to CMS, they are performing exceptionally well.

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:50.330
They are providing the care.

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:52.250
They are hitting the 85-cent rule.

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:54.490
They are getting top tier star ratings.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:58.090
You really have to wonder if the study was just made to please someone in a bureaucratic

00:09:58.090 --> 00:09:59.330
office somewhere.

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:00.330
Seriously.

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:04.690
Like someone who wasn't aware of the actual facts and the statutes imposed by the federal

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:05.690
government?

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:10.290
It completely ignores the reality that MA in Puerto Rico is filling massive gaps

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:13.650
that have been bypassed by Congress and the federal government for years.

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:14.650
Exactly.

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:17.810
The federal government under-funds the island by 38%.

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:23.770
The private MA plans step in, manage to get 99% of people on highly rated plans, and legally

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:26.450
spend the required 85% on care.

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:30.690
And then this study acts like the insurers are the bad guys for figuring out how to

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:31.930
survive that math.

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:34.650
Let's look at what this study actually complains about.

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:38.810
They say that when the funding gap widened, when Puerto Rico was getting less money

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:42.250
compared to the states, profits didn't automatically drop.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:43.250
Right.

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:47.610
And conversely, when the gap narrowed, profits didn't automatically rise.

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:54.450
For example, between 2016 and 2017, the funding disparity widened, things supposedly got tighter,

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:57.570
yet insurer profit margins actually increased.

00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:00.970
And the study frames this as deeply suspicious.

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:04.690
What's fascinating here is that the only thing the funding gap did consistently track

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:07.330
with was administrative spending.

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:11.970
So when federal funding got tight, they didn't cut patient care because they legally couldn't

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:16.570
and ethically shouldn't, they just shifted around their internal administrative spending.

00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:19.290
Which is just sound business management when you're underfunded.

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:20.290
Right.

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:24.770
But the study then pivots to this idea of disaster economics to explain the profit spikes.

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:27.450
Yeah, this is where they look at external shocks.

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:31.690
If you map out the 12 years of profit margins, you see these massive spikes that align with

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:33.210
national emergencies.

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:37.410
To understand why a disaster creates a profit spike for a health insurer, you

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:40.250
have to understand capitated payments.

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:41.250
Capitated meaning per head.

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:45.290
The government pays the insurer a flat fee per patient per month, regardless of

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:47.250
how much care that patient uses.

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:49.370
It's like having a monthly gym membership.

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:50.250
Oh, right.

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:53.570
You keep paying your fee, auto drafted from your account.

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:57.890
But suddenly a hurricane hits and the gym is closed for three months.

00:11:57.890 --> 00:12:01.970
The gym still gets your money, but they don't have to pay for electricity, water

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:03.450
or towel service.

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:06.770
Their costs vanish, but revenue stays the same.

00:12:06.770 --> 00:12:11.570
That perfectly captures the dynamic of Puerto Rico in 2017 with Hurricane Maria

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:14.290
and 2020 with the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:16.850
During Maria infrastructure was destroyed.

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:17.810
Roads were blocked.

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:19.050
The power grid collapsed.

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:21.650
Patients physically could not access their doctors.

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:25.170
And in 2020, you had lockdowns and quarantines.

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:27.530
Healthcare utilization plummeted.

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:30.890
Because MA operates on those capitated payments, the federal money

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:33.810
kept flowing in while the medical payouts dropped.

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:38.130
The study points out that in 2020, the medical loss ratio hit a study

00:12:38.130 --> 00:12:44.210
period low of 80.1 percent, while profit margins peaked at 3.32 percent.

00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:46.170
But again, let's be realistic here.

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:50.690
Are we supposed to blame the health plans because a global pandemic kept people home?

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:51.370
Exactly.

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:53.730
They were fully prepared to pay for the care.

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:56.530
The patients just literally couldn't go get it.

00:12:56.530 --> 00:13:01.010
And even then, they still maintained those 4.5 star ratings from CMS.

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:04.250
They were doing everything they could under impossible conditions.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:06.970
Here's where it gets really interesting, though.

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:10.090
Look at the final year of this study, 2022.

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:10.490
Right.

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:12.770
The pandemic lockdowns have subsided.

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:15.730
Healthcare utilization is returning to normal.

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:18.890
Crucially, this is also when these local Puerto Rican insurers

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:21.650
were acquired by national corporate firms.

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:23.090
What happened to the profits?

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:28.450
In 2022, the profit margin actually plunged to negative 0.19 percent.

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:30.850
The margins tighten up significantly

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:33.650
when the system operates under normal conditions.

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:36.690
So if we step back from the academic hand-ringing of the study,

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:38.730
what does this all actually mean?

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:41.890
What are the real policy recommendations here?

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:43.970
Well, let's finish strong with recommendations,

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:45.890
breaking it down for the federal government,

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:48.810
the private sector, and the beneficiaries.

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:50.450
Let's start with the federal government.

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:53.370
The researchers warn that just throwing more money at the system

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:56.130
without oversight might just inflate administrative costs.

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:56.970
Right.

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:00.130
If CMS is going to increase funding for equity,

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:01.650
they need strict oversight.

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:05.970
They suggest raising the MLR floor from 85% to 90%

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:08.010
for any additional funds.

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:10.290
I mean, that's fair if the feds finally step up

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:12.370
and close that 38% gap.

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:15.290
Ring fencing the new money to guarantee it goes to patients

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:16.570
makes sense.

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:18.090
But the federal government also needs

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:21.010
to recognize the reality that these MA plans are currently

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:22.930
holding the entire system together.

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:23.930
Exactly.

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:25.610
You can't regulate them into the ground

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:27.530
when they are the ones filling the mass of health care

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:29.010
gaps Congress has ignored.

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:31.490
Now, for the private sector, the study

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:34.370
recommends regulating vertical integration.

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:35.810
Right, because a lot of these insurers

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:38.370
own the provider networks and pharmacies they pay.

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:40.490
The government needs to strictly regulate what

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:43.570
counts as a medical expense when an insurer is essentially

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:45.210
paying its own subsidiary.

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:46.810
Keep the accounting transparent.

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:49.090
Prove the money is reaching the front line of care.

00:14:49.090 --> 00:14:50.970
And finally, for the beneficiaries.

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:52.890
The everyday people paying the premiums.

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:55.290
The recommendation is to strengthen traditional Medicare

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:57.170
to create actual competition.

00:14:57.170 --> 00:14:59.370
If the government reduces out-of-pocket costs

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:02.610
for traditional Medicare, making it a viable alternative,

00:15:02.610 --> 00:15:04.610
seniors would actually have a choice

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:06.610
outside of the private insurers.

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:08.570
Competition forces everyone to be better.

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:10.810
But if we connect this to the bigger picture,

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:13.650
it reflects a vulnerability in capitated health care

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:15.130
models nationwide.

00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:17.210
Right, Puerto Rico is just the extreme example

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:19.770
because of that 95% market penetration.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:23.570
Whenever you pay a private entity a flat fee to manage care,

00:15:23.570 --> 00:15:25.130
the financial incentive is always

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:27.370
going to be at a limit utilization.

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:28.890
But to summarize our deep dive today,

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:31.330
yes, Puerto Rico's MA system is undeniably

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:32.890
underfunded by the federal government.

00:15:32.890 --> 00:15:33.610
Absolutely.

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:34.770
That gap is real.

00:15:34.770 --> 00:15:36.490
But despite the study trying to paint

00:15:36.490 --> 00:15:38.530
the insurers as chaotic or illegal,

00:15:38.530 --> 00:15:41.450
the data shows they are legally hitting their 85-cent mandates

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:44.490
and providing 4.5-star care according to CMS.

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:46.170
The profit spikes during disasters

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:48.770
were a mechanical result of the capitated system

00:15:48.770 --> 00:15:51.650
during a crisis, not some malicious plot.

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:53.650
It really highlights how we need to look critically

00:15:53.650 --> 00:15:55.570
at academic studies that might just be pushing

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:57.970
a bureaucratic narrative rather than acknowledging

00:15:57.970 --> 00:15:59.610
the real work being done on the ground

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:01.530
to fill the gaps left by Congress.

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:03.530
And I'll leave you with a final thought to ponder

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:04.650
as we wrap up.

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:05.330
Let's hear it.

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:08.610
If a health care financing system, like Capitation,

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:11.650
is designed in such a way that it inherently achieves

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:14.690
its highest profitability during a population's greatest

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:18.650
periods of suffering, like a hurricane or a pandemic,

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:20.970
is the system fundamentally broken,

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:23.730
or is it working exactly as its financial incentives

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:25.490
were mathematically designed to?

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:28.210
Something to mull over next time you pay that premium.

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:29.730
Thanks for joining us on this deep dive,

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:31.450
and we'll catch you next time.

